Regularizers in Math RLVR (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01937v1)\nciteturn2367view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.01937v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 167\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 2 Nov 2025 (this version), latest version 9 Jan 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Shorter but not Worse: Frugal Reasoning via Easy Samples as Length Regularizers in Math RLVR\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Abdelaziz Bounhar , cite7†Hadi Abdine , cite8†Evan Dufraisse , cite9†Ahmad Chamma , cite10†Amr Mohamed , cite11†Dani Bouch , cite12†Michalis Vazirgiannis , cite13†Guokan Shang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Shorter but not Worse: Frugal Reasoning via Easy Samples as Length Regularizers in Math RLVR, by Abdelaziz Bounhar and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large language models (LLMs) trained for step-by-step reasoning often become excessively verbose, raising inference cost. Standard Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR) pipelines filter out ``easy'' problems for training efficiency, leaving the model to train primarily on harder problems that require longer reasoning chains. This skews the output length distribution upward, resulting in a \\textbf{model that conflates ``thinking longer'' with ``thinking better''}.\nL17: In this work, we show that retaining and modestly up-weighting moderately easy problems acts as an implicit length regularizer. Exposing the model to solvable short-chain tasks constrains its output distribution and prevents runaway verbosity. The result is \\textbf{\\emph{emergent brevity for free}}: the model learns to solve harder problems without inflating the output length, \\textbf{ despite the absence of any explicit length penalization}.\nL18: RLVR experiments using this approach on \\textit{Qwen3-4B-Thinking-2507} (with a 16k token limit) achieve baseline pass@1 AIME25 accuracy while generating solutions that are, on average, nearly twice as short. The code is available at \\href{cite16†this https URL†github.com }{GitHub}, with datasets and models on \\href{cite17†this https URL†huggingface.co }{Hugging Face}.\nL19: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (stat.ML)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite18†arXiv:2511.01937 [cs.LG]\nL21:    | (or cite19†arXiv:2511.01937v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL22:    | cite20†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.01937†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Abdelaziz Bounhar [cite21†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Sun, 2 Nov 2025 17:29:16 UTC (1,218 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Fri, 9 Jan 2026 15:02:00 UTC (1,734 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Shorter but not Worse: Frugal Reasoning via Easy Samples as Length Regularizers in Math RLVR, by Abdelaziz Bounhar and 7 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite14†View PDF L36:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite22†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite23†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.LG\nL43: \nL44: cite24†< prev |   cite25†next > L45: \nL46: cite26†new | cite27†recent | cite28†2025-11 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite29†cs L51: cite30†cs.AI L52: cite31†stat L53: cite32†stat.ML L54: \nL55: ### References & Citations\nL56: \nL57:   * cite33†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L58:   * cite34†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L59:   * cite35†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L60: \nL61: [Button: export BibTeX citation